     "result_text_tail": "ion.\n\n**Choices I made without asking you:**\n- **Theme:** `navy`.\n- **Navigation:** no section-divider pages; every page carries a five-section tracker instead.\n- **Executive summary:** it keeps the source's own sub-point structure, which was the only way to fit it at readable size.\n- **Board date:** shown as \"February 2027\". The pack gives no exact day, so I didn't invent one.\n- **Layout changes:** three pages changed form but not content. The e-commerce economics table on page 9 became two bridge charts, the one-time cost table on page 17 became a bar chart against the $25M cap, and the page 16 roadmap became a timeline with the FY2026 figures beside it.\n\n**Checks and remaining warnings.** The QA script reports 0 failures. The layout checker reports no problems on any page. I couldn't look at the slides as images (no LibreOffice here), so it's worth a quick visual pass in PowerPoint. 15 warnings remain, and I left them on purpose:\n- Several titles run 22\u201325 words, and a few pages are dense. Shortening further would drop the analysis's qualifiers or content.\n- On pages 15\u201317 the target, the FY2026 figures and the contingency sit beside the exhibit rather than being calculated from it.\n- Tables make up a large share of the deck, as they do in the source.\n\nThe spec that generates the deck is `work/harlow-margin-plan.json`. Next I can switch the theme, or answer any of the unverified numbers differently if you have the inputs, for example the CFO's actual one-time cost figures."
